sentences.l:
for by their very own wanton-follies they perished,
fools, who down the cattle of Hyperion the Sun
ate;

knowing the steep destruction, since beforehand to him we told, we ourselves,
Hermes having sent, the keen-sighted Argeïphontes,
neither the man himself to kill nor to woo his wife;

His daughter holds back the wretched, lamenting man,
and always with soft and coaxing words
she enchants him, so that Ithaca he will forget;

sentences.t:
οὔ νύ τ’ Ὀδυσσεὺς
Ἀργείων παρὰ νηυσὶ χαρίζετο ἱερὰ ῥέζων
Τροίῃ ἐν εὐρείῃ;

sentences.tr:
ou ny t’ Odysseus
Argeiōn para nēusi charizeto hiera rhezōn
Troiēi en eureiēi;

sentences.t:
πῶς ἂν ἔπειτ’ Ὀδυσῆος ἐγὼ θείοιο λαθοίμην,
ὃς περὶ μὲν νόον ἐστὶ βροτῶν, περὶ δ’ ἱρὰ θεοῖσιν
ἀθανάτοισιν ἔδωκε, τοὶ οὐρανὸν εὐρὺν ἔχουσιν;

sentences.tr:
pōs an epeit’ Odysēos egō theioio lathoimēn,
hos peri men noon esti brotōn, peri d’ hira theoisin
athanatoisin edōke, toi ouranon euryn echousin;

sentences.l:
But Poseidon the earth-holder, stubbornly, always,
for the Cyclops' sake is angered, whom of his eye he blinded,
godlike Polyphemus, whose strength is greatest
among all the Cyclopes;
